<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Bias | Md Fahim Sikder</title><link>https://fahimsikder.com/tags/bias/</link><atom:link href="https://fahimsikder.com/tags/bias/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Bias</description><generator>Hugo Blox Builder (https://hugoblox.com)</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 03 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://fahimsikder.com/media/icon_hu_51d43eb3b88ae3d9.png</url><title>Bias</title><link>https://fahimsikder.com/tags/bias/</link></image><item><title>What Makes an Algorithm Fair? Bias, Fairness, Representation Learning and Synthetic Data</title><link>https://fahimsikder.com/events/fairness-algorithm-swecss/</link><pubDate>Wed, 03 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fahimsikder.com/events/fairness-algorithm-swecss/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I gave an invited talk titled &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;What Makes an Algorithm Fair? Bias, Fairness, Representation Learning and Synthetic Data&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt; at the &lt;strong&gt;SweCSS Interdisciplinary Dialogue on Inequality and Fairness in AI and Machine Learning&lt;/strong&gt;, organised by the Swedish Excellence Centre for Computational Social Science.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The talk introduced algorithmic bias and fairness, how fairness can be measured, and why a model can look fair for each protected attribute on its own yet be unfair where they intersect. I then presented our approaches to tackling this: fair synthetic data generation and fair representation learning.&lt;/p&gt;
